Feature: Isolated synthetic tenant, authentication and session state
  C022 is a local, synthetic H005 fixture, not the approved Node 24 Express control API and not an identity service. Three fictional
  tenants (tenant-alder, tenant-birch, tenant-cedar) share ONE loopback server. Each has its own users, roles, sessions, projects,
  profiles and audit trail. Users: ada (admin), alf (member), ana (viewer) in alder; bea, ben, bo in birch; cai, cy in cedar; dana belongs
  to alder (member) and birch (viewer); nomad belongs to no tenant. An in-process stand-in for the identity provider (issueTicket) hands
  out one-time login tickets: nothing here is a real credential, there is no Entra, no cloud and no spend.
  The tenant of a request is derived ONLY from the verified session. A tenant named by a client in any header, query, path or body is
  refused (400 TENANT_CLAIM_REJECTED, whatever its value). Every read, list, search, mutation, cursor and ETag is scoped by tenant and the
  answer to "someone else's id" is byte for byte the answer to "no such id". The decision is repeated where the effect is committed.
  The shared browser helper permits only GET and HEAD: the real-browser observation shows reads and probes; every POST, PUT and DELETE
  is proven with real loopback sockets in Node.

  Scenario Outline: The tenant comes only from the verified session, never from a client claim
    Given "<user>" of tenant "<tenant>" holds an active session
    When the request carries the claim "<claim>" naming the OTHER tenant or the own one
    Then the answer is HTTP 400 with code "TENANT_CLAIM_REJECTED", the connection is closed and nothing changed
    Examples:
      | user | tenant       | claim                                             |
      | ada  | tenant-alder | header X-Tenant                                   |
      | ada  | tenant-alder | header X-Forwarded-Tenant                         |
      | ben  | tenant-birch | header Tenant-Id                                  |
      | ada  | tenant-alder | query ?tenant=                                    |
      | ben  | tenant-birch | query ?orgId=                                     |
      | ada  | tenant-alder | body field tenant on POST /v1/projects            |
      | alf  | tenant-alder | body field tenantId on PUT /v1/projects/proj-0001 |

  Scenario Outline: A cross-tenant probe on every kind and verb is answered exactly like a missing id, with no effect
    Given tenant-alder owns "proj-0001" and tenant-birch owns "proj-0001" (the same id, other content)
    When a member of one tenant sends "<verb> <path>" for an id that exists only in the OTHER tenant
    Then the answer is HTTP 404 "RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND" and its bytes (status, headers except Date, body) equal the answer for an id that exists nowhere
    And the world snapshot of both tenants is unchanged and the foreign tenant's audit trail gained nothing
    Examples:
      | verb   | path                   |
      | GET    | /v1/projects/<id>      |
      | PUT    | /v1/projects/<id>      |
      | DELETE | /v1/projects/<id>      |
      | GET    | /v1/profiles/<profile> |
      | POST   | /v1/admin/revoke (sid) |
      | POST   | /v1/admin/role (user)  |

  Scenario: The same resource id in two tenants never collides or leaks
    When both tenants hold "proj-0001" and each edits and deletes its own
    Then each tenant reads only its own content and ETag and the other is unaffected

  Scenario: Lists, counts, pagination and search show only the caller's tenant
    When members of tenants with 3, 2 and 0 projects list, page and search
    Then every item, count and cursor belongs to the caller's tenant and a cursor of another tenant is refused as CURSOR_INVALID

  Scenario: Conditional requests and caches cannot cross tenants
    Then every answer of the server's own handler says "Cache-Control: no-store" and "Vary: Authorization" (the answers Node gives itself say no-store only)
    And an ETag taken from tenant A never validates anything in tenant B (200, not 304) and an If-Match from A fails in B as 412
    And the server never sets or reads a cookie

  Scenario: The audit trail is per tenant and per user, admin only
    Then a denial or probe is recorded in the caller's tenant only, anonymous refusals in a separate ring, and one user's flood cannot evict another's records

  Scenario: Resource quota exhaustion in one tenant cannot starve another
    When tenant-cedar fills its project quota and its session quota
    Then further creates and logins in cedar are refused 409 and tenant-alder and tenant-birch still create and log in

  Scenario: Anonymous to authenticated changes the session id (no fixation)
    Given an anonymous session with token A
    When the user logs in with a valid ticket while presenting A
    Then the answer carries a NEW token B and a new session id, A is dead (SESSION_SUPERSEDED), and a client-chosen id is refused

  Scenario Outline: Illegal identity and session transitions fail with a typed refusal and no effect
    When <attempt>
    Then the answer is HTTP <status> with code "<code>" and the world snapshot is unchanged
    Examples:
      | attempt                                                | status | code                         |
      | a login ticket is replayed after its first use         | 401    | TICKET_INVALID               |
      | an expired or never issued ticket is presented         | 401    | TICKET_INVALID               |
      | nomad (no tenant) redeems a ticket                     | 403    | NOT_A_MEMBER                 |
      | an active session token is presented to login          | 409    | ALREADY_AUTHENTICATED        |
      | logout is repeated with the dead token                 | 401    | SESSION_LOGGED_OUT           |
      | a revoked token is used                                | 401    | SESSION_REVOKED              |
      | an expired token is used                               | 401    | SESSION_EXPIRED              |
      | a token is altered in any one character                | 401    | TOKEN_UNKNOWN                |
      | an anonymous token reaches a tenant route              | 401    | LOGIN_REQUIRED               |
      | an active session asks to switch tenant                | 403    | TENANT_SWITCH_REQUIRES_LOGIN |
      | an admin revokes a session that already ended          | 409    | SESSION_NOT_ACTIVE           |
      | an admin demotes the last admin of the tenant          | 409    | LAST_ADMIN                   |
      | a viewer sends a mutation                              | 403    | ROLE_FORBIDDEN               |
      | a body names role, owner, id or version                | 400    | INPUT_UNKNOWN_FIELD          |

  Scenario: A role change takes effect at once and an old token never keeps the old privilege
    When an admin demotes a member to viewer
    Then the member's EXISTING token is refused every mutation at once; after a re-upgrade the same token acts by the CURRENT role only

  Scenario: Tenant switch needs a fresh authentication
    Given dana holds an alder session
    When dana asks to switch, or sends a tenant claim, or presents the alder token for birch resources
    Then every attempt is refused with no effect; only a new login with a ticket for birch gives a separate birch session, and the alder session is untouched

  Scenario: Races are decided where the effect is committed
    Then of two logins with one ticket exactly one wins, a session that expires or is revoked while its request is queued has no effect, and a logout racing a mutation leaves a consistent state

  Scenario: Every refusal ends the connection and nothing runs behind it
    Then EVERY refusal status, first or later on a kept-alive connection, after an await, and every answer Node gives itself (417, no Host, parse error, CONNECT) is answered once, then the connection is closed
    And a valid mutation pipelined behind a refusal, behind one, two or three requests, in HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/1.0, in this process and in a separate one, never applies
    And 128 sockets each sending a GET, a GET and an unknown Expect leave no open socket and a legitimate GET is answered

  Scenario: A seeded random sequence keeps the global invariant after every operation
    Then no identity observes or changes the state of a tenant it is not authenticated into and every refused transition had no effect

  Scenario: The inspection page shows allowed and refused outcomes re-derived from facts
    Given two users of two tenants paste their tokens
    Then the page issues real fetches and shows, per probe, the observed status and typed code and an isolation verdict computed by comparing facts, never server prose
